  "two_of_them_were_wrong_first": "ADMIT_ITALIC_TOO only widened a regex, which could not reach the `underline in token` test - an injection that does not reach its target says nothing about the suite. And ROUTE_D01_TO_D02S_SUCCESSOR does not do that: making the branch unreachable falls through to the frozen v1 chain, which is the more dangerous outcome and which every name comparison misses. That hole was real and is now closed by a case that asks the routed API for Marriott's candidate."
 },
 "not_yet_reflected_in_a_batch": "The three Marriott D01 coordinates stay registered as defects with repair_state RULE_FIXED_RESULT_NOT_YET_RECOMPUTED. The committed batch ran on the previous closure and is the evidence for that one; releasing a defect requires naming the repaired result's id and the closure that produced it, and those come from a batch on this closure.",
 "record_type": "ISSUE_47_D01_UNDERLINE_REPAIR",
 "scoped_to_d01": {
  "the_link_guard_is_not_installed": "`not linked` on an audit-report opening - the check the sibling rule in the same module already makes - would make the shared widening safe. It is not added, because with the successor parser on D01's route alone it moves nothing today and a change that moves nothing is not exercised by anything. Measured and recorded in content-read.json rather than built.",
  "what_this_leaves_unrepaired": "Lumen's D02 case-name caption 'Blum', which the same shared widening would have added. It stays registered.",
  "why": "D02 and C02 keep the frozen parser, so nothing they select moves and audit_report_spans never sees an underline-marked block. Admitting underline on the shared route was measured and is not safe: it opens an audit-report exclusion on Southwest's hyperlinked index line that runs 849 blocks and takes five confirmed legal-contingency ones with it."
 },
